doc.add_page_break()

def generate_chapter(title, sections):
    p = doc.add_paragraph()
    p.alignment = WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER
    add_centered_run(p, f"{title}\n\n", 18, True)
    for section_title, section_text in sections:
        doc.add_heading(section_title, level=2)
        for para in section_text.split('\n\n'):
            if para.startswith("[FIGURE"):
                p = doc.add_paragraph()
                p.alignment = WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER
                for _ in range(5): doc.add_paragraph()
                r = p.add_run(para)
                r.italic = True
                for _ in range(5): doc.add_paragraph()
            else:
                p = doc.add_paragraph(para)
                p.alignment = WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.JUSTIFY
                p.paragraph_format.line_spacing = 1.5

ch1 = [
    ("1.1 Background and Context", '''Urbanization has been one of the most prominent demographic trends of the 21st century. As more people move to cities, the demand for infrastructure, housing, and mobility solutions has skyrocketed. Among these mobility challenges, parking has emerged as a critical issue. Studies indicate that up to 30% of traffic in urban downtown areas is generated by drivers actively searching for a parking spot. This 'cruising for parking' phenomenon not only contributes to severe traffic congestion but also leads to increased greenhouse gas emissions, wasted fuel, and significant economic losses due to lost time and productivity.

The traditional approach to managing parking facilities involves manual ticketing and physical inspection by parking attendants. In larger or more modern facilities, this has been upgraded to include physical hardware sensors—such as ultrasonic, magnetic inductive loops, or infrared sensors—installed in each individual parking slot. These hardware sensors detect the physical presence of a vehicle above them and relay this information to a central database.

However, these hardware-centric solutions present massive scalability and maintenance challenges. A commercial parking lot might contain over 5,000 slots. Installing 5,000 individual ultrasonic sensors requires massive capital investment. Furthermore, these sensors are exposed to the elements, physical damage from vehicles, and battery degradation, necessitating frequent, costly maintenance. They are also entirely single-purpose: an ultrasonic sensor can only detect whether a mass is above it; it cannot identify the vehicle, recognize a license plate, or detect security threats.

[FIGURE 1.1: Traditional Parking Infrastructure vs. Modern Camera Arrays]

In contrast, the proliferation of cheap, high-definition Closed-Circuit Television (CCTV) cameras presents a highly attractive alternative. A single strategically placed HD camera can monitor dozens of parking slots simultaneously. Furthermore, cameras provide rich visual data that can be used for multiple purposes: occupancy detection, vehicle identification (via ANPR), and security surveillance. The challenge, however, lies in analyzing this vast amount of visual data accurately and in real time. This is where Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Deep Learning come into play.'''),
    ("1.2 The Role of Intelligent Transportation Systems", '''Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) are an integral component of the Smart City vision. ITS leverages advanced communication technologies, IoT (Internet of Things), and data analytics to optimize traffic flow, improve road safety, and enhance the overall commuting experience.

Smart Parking is a critical subset of ITS. A fully realized Smart Parking system does not merely tell the operator how many spaces are empty; it actively guides the user to the nearest available space, manages their entry and exit seamlessly without manual ticketing, and processes payments automatically. To achieve this level of autonomy, the system must be completely frictionless.

Contactless operation has become especially paramount in recent years. By eliminating physical interactions at boom barriers, the flow of traffic into and out of a facility is greatly accelerated. This requires a highly robust Automatic Number Plate Recognition (ANPR) pipeline that can identify incoming vehicles instantly and accurately, logging their entry time and assigning them a vacant slot based on the live occupancy matrix.

[FIGURE 1.2: Architecture of an Intelligent Transportation System]'''),
    ("1.3 Smart Parking Evolution", '''The evolution of parking management can be categorized into three distinct generations.

First Generation: Purely manual operations. Drivers take a physical paper ticket from an attendant upon entry. Occupancy is estimated roughly by counting tickets, and drivers must visually search for empty slots. The system is slow, highly inaccurate, and creates massive bottlenecks at entry and exit gates.

Second Generation: Sensor-based automated systems. This generation introduced hardware boom barriers that dispense RFID or barcode tickets automatically. Ultrasonic sensors installed above or below each slot provide granular occupancy data. While a significant improvement over manual methods, these systems still require physical ticketing (which can be lost) and entail massive installation and maintenance costs for the sensor grids.

Third Generation: Vision-based, AI-driven contactless systems. The current cutting-edge standard. Cameras replace all per-slot sensors. Advanced Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) analyze the video feeds to determine occupancy. ANPR completely replaces ticketing; the vehicle's license plate becomes its unique identifier throughout its stay. This generation offers the highest scalability, lowest per-slot maintenance cost, and fastest throughput.

The project detailed in this report focuses strictly on advancing the Third Generation of parking systems by resolving its most persistent vulnerabilities: accuracy under poor environmental conditions and OCR hallucination on foreign license plates.'''),
    ("1.4 Computer Vision in Traffic Management", '''Computer Vision is the field of artificial intelligence that trains computers to interpret and understand the visual world. Using digital images from cameras and videos, deep learning models can accurately identify and classify objects, reacting to what they 'see.'

In traffic management, computer vision is primarily utilized for vehicle detection, tracking, speed estimation, and license plate recognition. Historically, computer vision relied on classical image processing techniques. Algorithms like Background Subtraction, Edge Detection (Canny/Sobel), and Haar Cascades were used to detect the presence of vehicles.

While computationally inexpensive, these classical methods proved highly fragile in unconstrained outdoor environments. A background subtraction algorithm might correctly identify a car on a sunny day but fail completely when the asphalt is wet and reflective, or when a passing cloud casts a large shadow over the parking lot. These algorithms lack semantic understanding; they only detect pixel-level changes.

[FIGURE 1.3: Classical Computer Vision vs. Deep Learning Feature Maps]

Deep Learning, specifically Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), revolutionized this space. Instead of relying on hand-crafted pixel rules, CNNs learn hierarchical feature representations directly from raw data. The lower layers of a CNN learn to detect edges and textures, while deeper layers learn to recognize complex geometric structures like wheels, windshields, and chassis profiles. This semantic understanding makes CNNs incredibly robust to environmental noise. A CNN knows that a car is a car, regardless of whether a shadow is cast across its hood.'''),
    ("1.5 Problem Statement", '''Identifying the occupancy status of individual parking slots in real-world scenarios presents massive challenges due to varying environmental conditions. Open-air parking lots are subjected to extreme weather (heavy rain, dense fog, snow accumulation), dynamic lighting (blinding sun glare, long shadows, low-light nighttime conditions), and unusual camera angles.

Traditional image processing techniques and lightweight, shallow neural networks often fail to maintain high accuracy under these unpredictable conditions, resulting in false positives (e.g., classifying a shadow as a car) and false negatives (e.g., failing to detect a black car at night). This necessitates a highly robust deep transfer-learning approach capable of processing multi-source visual inputs reliably.

Furthermore, the entry and exit management aspect of smart parking relies heavily on Automatic Number Plate Recognition (ANPR). A major flaw in existing ANPR systems deployed in parking lots is their inability to handle diverse typography. Deep learning OCR models, such as LPRNet, are typically trained on highly specific regional datasets (e.g., Indian license plates). While they achieve exceptional accuracy on their training distribution, they exhibit severe 'hallucination' behaviors when presented with out-of-distribution international plates, forcing the model to output characters that simply aren't there to satisfy its learned structural templates. Conversely, generalized OCR models lack the precision required for regional formats. There is a critical need for a dynamic, hybrid OCR pipeline capable of recognizing varied global plate formats without manual intervention or region toggling.'''),
    ("1.6 Project Objectives", '''To address the identified problems and develop a comprehensive, state-of-the-art parking management solution, this project outlines the following core objectives:

1. Occupancy Detection Model: To develop and train a highly accurate binary classification model utilizing VGG16 transfer learning. The model must accurately determine individual parking slot occupancy (vacant vs. occupied) regardless of environmental noise.

2. Multi-Source Ingestion: To engineer a flexible backend pipeline capable of supporting multi-source visual inputs. The system must seamlessly process live RTSP CCTV feeds, uploaded video files (mp4, avi), and static images (jpg, png).

3. Benchmark Validation: To rigorously train and validate the VGG16 model on massive standard benchmark datasets including PKLot, CNRPark, and CNRPark-EXT, alongside a custom-curated SOCIETY_PARKING dataset, with the goal of achieving near 99.9% accuracy.

4. Hybrid OCR Pipeline: To invent and implement a novel Hybrid OCR Engine that effectively fuses LPRNet (for high-precision regional plates) and EasyOCR (for global robustness). The engine must utilize regex-based structural heuristics to automatically detect the plate format and route the image to the appropriate decoding logic, eliminating hallucination entirely.

5. Decentralized Edge Architecture: To design and implement a decentralized processing framework where computationally expensive CNN inference is executed locally on edge nodes (e.g., at the camera site). This ensures only lightweight JSON state updates are transmitted to the cloud, eliminating network bandwidth bottlenecks.

6. Real-Time Web Dashboard: To construct a highly responsive, React-based web application that connects to the FastAPI backend via WebSockets. The dashboard must provide administrators and users with a Live HUD, visualizing the global occupancy matrix and facilitating seamless vehicle entry and exit management.''')
]
